Chess Keyboard Edition – Project Requirements Document
1. Overview

Build a browser-based JavaScript chess game where:

Human plays against the computer.

Board is a 2D overhead view using standard chess piece symbols.

Input is keyboard-driven:

Left/Right arrows: cycle through selectable pieces or destination squares.

Enter: confirm selection (first press selects a piece; second press confirms destination).

Escape: cancel current move selection and reset to neutral state.

Left side of the screen displays a scrolling tape of moves for both players.

The engine is “casual strong”: good enough to beat most non-obsessed humans, nowhere near modern engine strength.

Target environment: modern desktop browser (Chrome/Firefox/Edge) running JavaScript + HTML/CSS. No backend required.

2. Goals

Provide a fully legal chess implementation (including castling, en passant, promotion, check/checkmate detection).

Implement a keyboard-only control scheme suitable for accessibility and “no-mouse” play.

Offer an AI that:

Punishes obvious blunders.

Demonstrates basic tactics (captures, forks, not hanging pieces every move).

Provide a clear, readable move history on the left side.

3. Non-Goals

No online multiplayer, matchmaking, or networking.

No opening books, endgame tablebases, or advanced engine features.

No sophisticated animations or 3D visuals.

No time controls or clocks (unless added later as an enhancement).

No engine vs engine mode.

4. Game Rules & Mechanics
4.1 Standard Chess Rules

The game must implement standard FIDE chess rules:

8×8 board, standard initial setup for White and Black.

Turn-based play: White moves first, then alternate turns.

Legal moves enforced for all piece types:

Pawn (including initial double-step, diagonal capture).

Knight.

Bishop.

Rook.

Queen.

King.

4.2 Special Rules

Castling:

King and rook haven’t moved.

No pieces between king and rook.

King is not in check, does not pass through check, and does not land in check.

En passant:

Track last move to determine en passant availability.

Only available immediately after an opposing pawn moves two squares.

Pawn Promotion:

When a pawn reaches the last rank:

At minimum, auto-promote to queen.

(Optional enhancement: allow user to select promotion piece via keyboard.)

Check / Checkmate / Stalemate:

Detect check: king is under attack.

Detect checkmate: king is in check and no legal moves.

Detect stalemate: king is not in check and no legal moves.

Other draw rules:

Insufficient material (basic detection: K vs K, K+minor vs K, etc.).

Threefold repetition (optional; nice-to-have).

Fifty-move rule (optional; nice-to-have).

4.3 Illegal Move Handling

Prevent pseudo-legal moves that:

Move pieces contrary to their movement rules.

Move through other pieces (where not allowed).

Leave own king in check.

If the player attempts an illegal move (e.g., presses Enter on an invalid destination):

Play an error sound or show a subtle message.

Remain in “select destination” state so they can choose another square or press Escape to cancel.

5. User Interface
5.1 Layout

Screen layout (desktop, landscape):

Left Panel (Move Tape):

Fixed width column (e.g., ~25–30% of viewport width).

Displays move history for both sides.

Right Panel (Game Area):

Chessboard centered.

Optional status area above or below board:

Current player to move.

Game status (e.g., “Check”, “Checkmate – White wins”, “Stalemate”).

5.2 Board Rendering

8×8 grid:

Alternating light/dark squares.

Simple, high-contrast theme.

Pieces:

2D standard piece icons (SVG, PNG, or Unicode piece characters).

Highlights:

Current selection cursor (square-by-square focus).

Selected piece (after first Enter press) clearly highlighted.

Optionally highlight legal destination squares for currently selected piece.

5.3 Move Tape (Left Side)

Vertical scrolling list displaying move history:

Simple algebraic notation (e.g., 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 ...), or a more explicit form if desired.

Requirements:

Show move number and both White/Black moves.

Auto-scroll so the latest move is visible.

Highlight the most recent move.

Advanced navigation (going back in time / move replay) is out-of-scope for v1, but the data model should not make it impossible later.

6. Input & Controls

The entire UX is driven primarily by the keyboard.

6.1 Selection Model

The board squares are internally represented as an ordered list (e.g., 0–63, A1 to H8 in reading order), and the control logic uses an index into that list.

States

Neutral State:

No piece selected.

A single square is focused (cursor position) and visibly highlighted.

Piece Selected State:

A specific piece belonging to the human player is selected.

The destination cursor cycles through legal destination squares for that piece.

Navigation Rules

Left Arrow:

Neutral state: Move focus one square backward in the 0–63 index (wrapping at edges).

Piece selected: Cycle backward through list of legal destination squares.

Right Arrow:

Neutral state: Move focus one square forward in the 0–63 index (wrapping).

Piece selected: Cycle forward through legal destination squares.

(Up/Down arrows may be ignored or used as future enhancements for more intuitive navigation; they’re not required.)

Enter Key Behavior

Neutral State:

If the focused square contains a piece of the current player, pressing Enter:

Transitions to Piece Selected State.

Stores the selected piece and its legal target squares.

If focused square is empty or contains an opponent piece:

Ignored or triggers a subtle “invalid selection” feedback.

Piece Selected State:

Enter confirms the currently focused destination square:

If the square is a valid destination:

Execute move.

Capture piece if destination contains an opponent piece.

Update board, move list, and game state.

Switch turn to AI.

If not a valid destination (shouldn’t normally happen if we only cycle legal moves):

Play error feedback and remain in state.

Escape Key Behavior

At any time during a move selection:

Escape cancels current move selection.

Return to Neutral State.

Focus may return to:

The original piece’s square, or

Last neutral focus position.

Escape never changes the game state, only the UI’s selection state.

7. Game Flow
7.1 New Game

On page load:

Initialize game in standard start position.

By default, human plays White (can be adjustable later).

Move tape starts empty.

Status shows: “New game – White to move”.

(Optional future setting: choose color: Human as White or Black.)

7.2 Human Turn

Player uses Left/Right to choose a piece.

Press Enter to select it.

Use Left/Right to cycle through legal destinations for that piece.

Press Enter to confirm move.

Board and move tape update.

Game checks for check, checkmate, or stalemate.

If the move ends the game:

Show final game result.

AI does not move.

7.3 AI Turn

Once the human move is applied and game is not over:

Generate list of legal moves for AI.

Run chess engine to evaluate and choose move.

Apply chosen move to board state.

Append AI move to move tape.

Update status (e.g., “Black to move”, “White is in check”).

Control returns to human.

8. AI / Chess Engine Requirements

This is not Stockfish. It’s a simple but not idiotic engine.

8.1 Engine Approach

Likely approach:

Minimax search with alpha-beta pruning.

Fixed depth (e.g., 2–3 plies full search, possibly depth extensions for checks/captures).

Must be performant enough in JavaScript in a browser for:

Instant or near-instant moves on a typical desktop.

If slow, the engine can:

Limit depth.

Use basic heuristics (e.g., prefer captures, avoid hanging pieces).

8.2 Evaluation Function

Material balance (piece values: pawn, knight, bishop, rook, queen, king).

Simple positional heuristics (optional, but recommended):

Development (pieces off starting ranks).

King safety (penalize exposed king).

Central control (pieces/pawns controlling central squares).

At minimum, engine should:

Not consistently blunder major pieces for no reason.

Recognize basic tactical captures and recaptures.

8.3 Difficulty

Single difficulty level in v1: “Casual”.

Code should be structured so future difficulties are easy:

“Easy”: shallow depth + randomization.

“Hard”: deeper depth.

9. Data Model & Architecture
9.1 Core Game State

Represent game state in a single object, e.g.:

board: 8×8 array or 64-element array of piece codes (e.g., "wP", "bK").

sideToMove: "white" or "black".

Castling rights (white king-side, white queen-side, black king-side, black queen-side).

enPassantSquare: index or null.

Half-move clock (for 50-move rule, optional).

Full move number.

Move history list (for supporting move tape and possible future undo).

9.2 Move Generation & Validation

Distinguish between:

Pseudo-legal move generation (ignores self-check).

Legal move filtering (removes moves that leave king in check).

Functions:

generateLegalMoves(state).

isInCheck(state, color).

applyMove(state, move) -> newState.

undoMove(state, move) (optional but very helpful for engine).

9.3 Rendering Layer

Pure functions or simple React/Vue/Svelte/vanilla JS (implementation detail) that:

Take game state and UI selection state.

Render board and move tape in DOM or canvas.

9.4 Input Layer

Keyboard event listeners bound to:

ArrowLeft, ArrowRight, Enter, Escape.

Translates key presses into updates of:

Selection cursor index.

Current selection state (neutral vs piece-selected).

Triggering move application.

10. Error Handling & UX Feedback

Illegal actions:

Trying to select an empty square or opponent piece in neutral state:

Slight shake animation or message: “No piece to move.”

Trying to confirm a move when selection state is invalid:

Ignore input gracefully.

Game over:

Once the game result is reached, further move attempts are ignored until a reset.

Display clear result message and a “New Game” button (clickable or mapped to key like N as a future enhancement).

11. Performance Considerations

Board and UI should update instantly for human move.

AI move computation should complete in a reasonable time on a typical desktop browser.

Techniques:

Keep game state small and immutable-friendly.

Avoid unnecessary DOM re-renders.

Possibly run engine in a Web Worker in later versions (out-of-scope for v1 but keep code modular to allow it).

12. Testing & Validation

Unit tests for:

Move generation (including edge cases for castling, en passant, promotion).

Check/checkmate/stalemate detection.

Basic AI tests:

Engine should not:

Leave its own king in check.

Consistently hang queens/rooks in 1 move.

Manual playtesting:

Full game from start to finish verifying:

Move tape correctness.

Keyboard control flow.

ESC cancels correctly.

Game end conditions.

13. Assumptions & Open Design Choices

These are decisions this document currently assumes (you can override them at implementation time):

Human plays White by default; color selection is a later enhancement.

Board is implemented via DOM + CSS (easier for highlighting and accessibility than canvas).

Move notation in the tape is algebraic but simplified (no check markers, etc., in v1) to keep implementation simpler.

Promotion defaults to queen (no choice UI in v1).

A natural next step after this PRD would be to break this into concrete implementation tasks: data structures, move generator, engine skeleton, and then the UI and input wiring.